---
// Project detail — one page per project in the portfolio, the screen the
// Projects index and the Recently viewed row have been linking at.
//
// A PROJECT IS A HUB, AND THE PAGE SORTS IT BY LINEAR'S TEST (user, 2026-10-22):
// things you READ go in the main column, things you SET go in the rail. The
// main column is the DOCUMENT — the description the sponsor wrote, the tracked
// tables (measures, expenditures, funding, milestones, in the order a reader
// judges delivery), the photos, the conversation. The rail is the PROPERTY
// SHEET — every value picked from a list or typed into a box (stage, type,
// classifications, tags, lead organization, county, timeline, cost), the
// shapes on the ground, the people on the record, the records this one links
// to. Linear's project page is exactly that split: an overview that opens with
// a property strip and the description, and a details sidebar that carries
// the full property list, members, relations and a progress graph.
//
// THIS REPLACED "SERIES VS SCALAR", which sorted by whether a thing had a
// history. It explained the tracked tables well and the rest badly: the
// description (a paragraph, read) was a scalar by that rule and spent two
// releases in a 22rem column; the comments (read) hid behind a tab; funding
// (a list you grow) had to be argued OUT of the rail on width alone. "Read it
// or set it" gives the same answer for the tables and the right one for the
// other three. Brief §4 records both rules and the screen that taught each.
//
// THE STRIP REPEATS THE RAIL, on purpose. Four properties — Stage, Timeline,
// Estimated cost, Lead organization — are what every number below is READ
// AGAINST, and the rail that holds them collapses on a button and unpins on a
// tablet. So the main column carries its own row of them under the title, as
// Linear's overview does, and the rail keeps the complete sheet. Both are the
// same component and the same editable rows; a commit in one repaints the
// other. This is also what makes the description's position affordable: the
// earlier reversal that sent it back to the rail was protecting the fold for
// the numbers' context, and the strip now holds that context above the fold
// whatever the paragraph's length.
//
// THE RAIL IS PINNED for the same reason it always was — availability, not
// emphasis. It scrolls inside itself when its stack outgrows the screen. Its
// sections are stacked rather than tabbed now: the tabs were paid for by three
// peers and a comment badge, and with comments in the document the two left
// would hide one short section for no gain.
//
// THE MAP IS IN THE RAIL, where Linear keeps its progress graph: the summary
// of the thing the main column tracks, beside the numbers rather than a
// screen below them. It is the whole work-areas component at its rail size —
// tools, forms and the area list included — because the work areas ARE the
// map; there is no second map for a thumbnail to stand in for. A screenful of
// map at the end of the column, which is what it was, cost the column its
// landing and bought the reader nothing a 18rem map beside the facts does not.
//
// WHO IT IS FOR. Not only the sponsor. An executive director checking whether
// their organization's project is on track, and a member of the public who
// found it from the index, land on the same page and both want the same
// question answered first: is this project doing what it said it would.
//
// IT IS ALSO A WORKFLOW ENTRY POINT, and since 2026-10-02 it has a VIEW and an
// EDIT mode (user-directed, the same pair custom pages carry; brief §4 records
// the reversal). It opens to read: every field read-only, the Add affordances
// and the work-area tools out of sight. Edit, beside the title, brings them
// back and becomes Update, which closes the edit — firma2-record-mode owns the
// switch, and its header says what stays available while reading (the
// reporting acts — Record work, Record spend — among them). The crumb row still
// carries the record-level actions that touch nothing on the record — Share,
// Fact sheet, History — see firma2-project-actions. The Add affordances are inert in this prototype — they
// render, focus, and take a click, and nothing happens. They are deliberately
// NOT `disabled`: a greyed-out control tells a reviewer the action is
// unavailable to them, which is a different and false claim than "not wired
// yet". Expenditures is the one section whose action is not an "Add": it says
// "Record spend", because a year of money is FILED against a period as that
// period closes, not appended to a list — and unlike the Add affordances it is
// live, writing to a browser-local store the same way Record work does. See
// that component's own header.
//
// ROUTE. getStaticPaths builds one page per project from `projects`, keyed by
// projectSlug — the SAME function projectHref() uses, so a link in the index or
// the recently-viewed row can never point at a slug this route did not
// generate. firma2-projects.ts throws at build on a slug collision, which is
// what keeps that guarantee honest as projects are added.
//
// ZERO PAGE CSS. Every section owns its own markup and styles; this file is the
// spine and the manifest, nothing else.
import AppLayout from '../../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import Firma2RecordRail from '../../../components/shared/firma2-record-rail.astro';
import Firma2ProjectRail from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-rail.astro';
import Firma2ProjectFacts from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-facts.astro';
import Firma2RailModes from '../../../components/shared/firma2-rail-modes.astro';
import Firma2RecordBar from '../../../components/shared/firma2-record-bar.astro';
import Firma2ProjectDescription from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-description.astro';
import Firma2ProjectComments from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-comments.astro';
import Firma2ProjectGisStatus from '../../../components/gis/firma2-project-gis-status.astro';
import Firma2ProjectMeasures from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-measures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectExpenditures from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-expenditures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectFunding from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-funding.astro';
import Firma2ProjectTimeline from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-timeline.astro';
import Firma2ProjectGallery from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-gallery.astro';
import Firma2ProjectActions from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-actions.astro';
import Firma2RecordMode from '../../../components/shared/firma2-record-mode.astro';
import Firma2ProjectEditAccess from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-edit-access.astro';
import Firma2ProjectPublishState from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-publish-state.astro';
import Firma2ProgramRequirements from '../../../components/projects/firma2-program-requirements.astro';
import { reviewHref } from '../../../data/firma2-publishing';
import { projects, projectSlug } from '../../../data/firma2-projects';
import type { Project } from '../../../data/firma2-projects';
import { getProjectDetail } from '../../../data/firma2-project-detail';
import { HYDROLOGIC_REGIONS, seedWorkAreas } from '../../../data/firma2-geography';
import { withBase } from '../../../lib/base';
import EsaContainer from '@esa/ecology/esa-container.astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return projects.map((project) => ({
    params: { project: projectSlug(project) },
    props: { project },
  }));
}

interface Props {
  project: Project;
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
const detail = getProjectDetail(project);
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: record bar, then sidebar[data-side=end] > [ rail · stack(xl) ]
  sections:
    - record bar             -> firma2-record-bar       (pinned under the app bar)
      - rail modes              -> firma2-rail-modes       (Details · Map; opens, closes, switches the rail)
    - rail (100dvh, sticky)  -> firma2-record-rail
      - rail stack              -> firma2-project-rail
        - details mode: key facts -> firma2-project-facts  (list layout)
        - details mode: contacts  -> firma2-project-contacts
        - map mode              -> firma2-work-areas       (rail size; editable; Falls within under the list)
    - page header            -> firma2-page-header      (sticky band)
      - edit / update           -> firma2-record-mode
      - publish state           -> firma2-project-publish-state (status row; unpublished changes)
      - record actions          -> firma2-project-actions
                                   (review changes · share · fact sheet · change-history sheet)
    - property strip         -> firma2-project-facts    (strip layout: stage · timeline · cost · lead)
    - description            -> firma2-project-description
    - performance measures   -> firma2-project-measures
    - expenditures           -> firma2-project-expenditures
    - funding sources        -> firma2-project-funding
    - program requirements   -> firma2-program-requirements (only on projects shared with a program)
    - milestones             -> firma2-project-timeline
    - photos                 -> firma2-project-gallery
    - comments               -> firma2-project-comments
  note: the page sorts by Linear's test (user, 2026-10-22) — things you READ
        go in the main column, things you SET go in the rail. The main column
        is the document: a strip of the four properties that identify the
        project, the description, the tracked tables, the conversation. The
        rail is the property sheet: every property, the map, the people, the
        links. The strip REPEATS four rail rows on purpose (Linear mirrors
        too): the rail collapses and unpins, and the main column must still
        carry what its numbers are read against;
        the rail carries two controls of its own — a collapse button at its head
        and a bcn-edge-handle on its leading edge that drags the track wider — so
        the width below is a default rather than a verdict;
        the page takes the full pane (width="full") and caps only the main
        column with an EsaContainer, so the rail holds the window's edge however
        wide the screen gets.
-->
<AppLayout title={`${project.projectName} — ProjectFirma 2.0`} active="projects" width="full">
  <!-- TWO COLUMNS FOR THE WHOLE PAGE, not for one section of it. `.sidebar` is
       the lego for a fixed rail beside a fluid main, and this is the outermost
       element on the page precisely so the rail is beside EVERYTHING — the
       header band included. That is the difference between a rail and a panel:
       the record does not sit next to the map, it sits next to the page.

       ITS FIRST DOM CHILD IS ALWAYS THE ASIDE, and data-side="end" orders it to
       the trailing edge, so the rail renders on the right while staying first in
       the source. Reversing the DOM to match the eye would mean fighting the
       primitive, which reads its aside off :first-child.

       THE PRIMITIVE'S OWN --gap, and it was briefly set to 0. That was solving
       the right problem the wrong way: the map and the rail are both meant to
       reach their edges, so a gutter between them would have been a stripe of
       page background running the full height of the screen. Zeroing the gap
       closed it — and took the gutter away from everything ELSE in the column
       too, so all four cards below the map butted straight into the rail with no
       margin at all.

       The gap belongs here; the map is the exception and it already handles
       itself. It bleeds --spacing-500 past its column on both edges, which is
       exactly this gap, so it crosses the gutter and meets the rail while the
       cards below it stop short of it by one step. One value, two behaviours,
       and neither is a special case bolted onto the layout. The header band does
       the same thing for the same reason.

       --sidebar-width: 25rem IS A TRACK WIDTH, and it is 3rem wider than the
       22rem the cards inside it want because the rail spends --spacing-500 of
       padding on each side out of that track. The bleed adds nothing to it — a
       negative margin lets a box overflow its track, it does not widen it — so
       the rail reaches the window's edge at the same 25rem it was given.
       firma2-record-rail states the same arithmetic where it is applied.

       --sidebar-content-min AS A LENGTH, the same choice and the same reason as
       the measure setup page's rail: the 60% default is a fraction of a container
       whose width already depends on the app nav's collapse state, so the wrap
       point moves for a reason that has nothing to do with whether the content
       still fits. 40rem is a floor for the main column stated in the same units
       the content is measured in. The rail unpins itself once that wrap
       happens — see its own media query, and the note there about why a viewport
       query is standing in for a container one.
       min(…, 100%) because a floor wider than the pane is not a floor: at
       ~900–970px the inset sheet's content box is narrower than 40rem, and the
       column overflowed into the sheet's right padding (user, 2026-10-21). -->
  <!-- THE RECORD'S OWN BAR, under the app's (user, 2026-10-22, after
       Linear): pinned, spanning the main column and the rail, with the rail's
       icons at its trailing end — Details and Map. Each shows the rail in its
       mode; the pressed one closes it. -->
  <Firma2RecordBar>
    <Firma2RailModes
      slot="end"
      rail="firma2-record-rail"
      storageKey="project-rail"
      modes={[
        { key: 'details', label: 'Details', icon: 'panel-right' },
        { key: 'map', label: 'Map', icon: 'map' },
      ]}
    />
  </Firma2RecordBar>
  <div
    class="sidebar"
    data-side="end"
    style="--sidebar-width: 25rem; --sidebar-content-min: min(40rem, 100%)"
  >
    <!-- THE PROPERTY SHEET, PINNED FOR AS LONG AS THE PAGE LASTS. Everything
         in it is SET — picked, typed, drawn, linked — and it is here rather
         than in the main column because a reader needs it to place the numbers
         without it being what the page exists to answer. Pinned, it never
         scrolls away from the numbers it is placing; stacked, it scrolls
         inside itself when the stack outgrows the screen. firma2-project-rail
         owns the order and the argument for stacking over tabs.
         THE WIDTH TEST STILL APPLIES. A rail row is short enough to read at a
         glance and narrow enough to live at 22rem; funding sources failed that
         and lives in the main column (see its placement note below), and the
         work-areas list in here drops a column to pass it. -->
    <Firma2RecordRail label="project details" external>
      <Firma2ProjectRail detail={detail} />
    </Firma2RecordRail>

  <!-- `xl`, not the 2xl this stack used to carry: it is the gap between the
       sticky header band and the first section, and xl is the rhythm the cards
       already keep between themselves — so the page reads as one interval
       throughout instead of a wider one at the top.

       THE BAND IS A DIRECT CHILD, and that is structural, not incidental: it is
       `position: sticky`, which is bounded by its parent, so anything wrapped
       around it would unpin it the moment that wrapper scrolled past. It stays a
       direct child of a stack as tall as the page. The map is the second child
       for a different reason — it bleeds past this stack's inline edges with a
       negative margin, which only reaches the pane's padding from a child of
       something full-width.

       THE BAND'S OWN TRAILING BLEED IS LEFT ALONE, and there is a hook for
       closing it (--firma2-band-bleed-end) that this page briefly used. It was
       needed while the sidebar's gap was 0: the band bleeds a step past its
       column, the column ended flush against the rail, and the band is sticky at
       --z-header while the rail sits a rung below at --z-sidebar — so the bleed
       painted a 24px notch of band background over the rail's top corner. With
       the gap restored the bleed lands exactly IN the gutter and stops on the
       rail's leading edge, which is where it should have been stopping all
       along. Nothing overlaps, and the band still reads as the top of the page
       rather than a card floating in it. -->
  <!-- 60rem, centred (user, 2026-10-22): the rail now closes to nothing from
       the top bar, so the column has to stop growing somewhere that still
       reads as a document — about Linear's width, with the tables' room. -->
  <!-- THE BARE <div> IS THE SCROLL BOX (firma2-record-bar): it takes the whole
       track, so the column's scrollbar meets the rail — or the pane's edge
       once the rail is closed — while the container centres inside it. -->
  <div>
  <EsaContainer maxWidth="60rem">
  <div class="stack" data-gap="xl">
    <!-- Two crumbs, not three. "Home > Projects > <name>" would spend a step on a
         route this app's sidenav already owns; the one thing a reader needs from
         here is the way back to the list they came from. The last crumb takes no
         href and esa-breadcrumbs marks it aria-current="page". Both crumb and
         title ride in the sticky band, so the way out stays on screen for the
         whole page rather than only for its first screenful. -->
    <!-- THE TITLE IS A FIELD, not a screen name. Every other stored value on this
         record can be corrected where it is read — lead organization, program,
         county, timeline, cost, the description — and the project's NAME is the
         one that was still read-only, which made it the odd field out on its own
         page. `editableTitle` hands it the same choreography: hover or focus the
         h1, the pencil appears beside it, Enter saves and Escape abandons. The
         breadcrumb below repeats the name and the header keeps it in step. -->
    <Firma2PageHeader
      title={project.projectName}
      editableTitle={{ field: 'projectName', label: 'Project name' }}
      crumbs={[
        { label: 'Projects', href: withBase('/prototypes/projects'), aliasable: true },
        { label: project.projectName },
      ]}
    >
      <!-- NO STAGE IN THE TOP BAR (user, 2026-10-19): the bar is chrome — the
           way out and the things you can do — and the stage is a fact about the
           record, so it leads the Key facts panel in the rail instead
           (firma2-project-facts). -->
      <!-- EDIT / UPDATE, BESIDE THE TITLE. The record opens to read and this
           is the one way into editing it: ghost Edit while reading, primary
           Update while editing — the button that starts the edit is the one
           that ends it. It rides the ACTIONS slot, beside the title, because
           it is workflow on THIS record; the crumb row below is chrome. An
           inert "Edit project" button once stood here and was deleted as a
           mode the app did not have (brief §4); since 2026-10-02 the app has
           the mode, and the button does what it says. -->
      <Firma2RecordMode slot="actions" noun="Project" />
      <!-- A reader who cannot edit this project (a Viewer, or a Contributor
           outside its lead organization) gets Request edit access in Edit's
           place — firma2-project-edit-access hides the switch above. -->
      <Firma2ProjectEditAccess
        slot="actions"
        project={project.projectName}
        leadOrganization={project.leadOrganization}
      />
      <!-- WHAT STANDS IN THE CRUMB ROW PASSES A DIFFERENT TEST: Share,
           Fact sheet and History touch nothing on the record. They hand the
           page to someone else, take it away as a file, or read what
           happened to it — actions on the WHOLE record with no section to
           belong to. They ride the TOOLBAR slot — the breadcrumb row, not
           the title's bar — because they are chrome, not workflow: the crumb
           strip is the page's frame (the way out on one edge, the things you
           can do with the page on the other), and the title stays the
           record's alone (user-directed 2026-08-25; one release at md beside
           the title first). The full argument, and the live/inert split, is
           firma2-project-actions' own header. -->
      <!-- The public page is a published snapshot; when the record has moved
           past it, the status row says so and the crumb row offers the diff
           (user, 2026-10-09 — the review-and-publish workflow). -->
      <Firma2ProjectPublishState slot="status" slug={projectSlug(project)} publishing={detail.publishing} />
      <Firma2ProjectActions
        slot="toolbar"
        audit={detail.audit}
        reviewHref={detail.publishing.changes.length ? withBase(reviewHref(project)) : undefined}
      />
    </Firma2PageHeader>

    <div class="stack" data-gap="xl">
      <!-- Hidden unless a GIS subscription stopped returning this project; also
           locks the Key facts a subscription keeps. -->
      <Firma2ProjectGisStatus project={project} />

      <!-- THE PROPERTY STRIP, under the title and ahead of everything. Linear's
           overview opens the same way: status, lead, dates in one row, then the
           document. These are the four facts the numbers below are READ
           AGAINST, and the rail that also carries them collapses on a button
           and unpins on a tablet — so the main column keeps its own copy. The
           same rows as the rail's, editable in both; a commit in one repaints
           the other. -->
      <Firma2ProjectFacts detail={detail} layout="strip" />

      <!-- THE DESCRIPTION, FIRST IN THE DOCUMENT. It is the one field on the
           record a reader READS rather than looks up, which is Linear's test
           for the main column, and it lands where Linear lands it: after the
           properties, before the tracked things. It sat under the title once
           and was sent back for pushing the numbers below the fold; the strip
           above is what makes the position affordable now — the facts the
           numbers are read against stay above the fold whatever its length. -->
      <Firma2ProjectDescription description={detail.projectDescription} />

      <!-- The work areas, regions and classifications let the section work
           out what it can (measured and estimated figures) and ask only the
           measures this project's classifications track. -->
      <Firma2ProjectMeasures
        measures={detail.measures}
        projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)}
        workAreas={seedWorkAreas(project)}
        regions={HYDROLOGIC_REGIONS}
        classifications={project.classifications}
      />
      <Firma2ProjectExpenditures
        expenditures={detail.expenditures}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
        estimatedAnnualOperatingCost={detail.project.estimatedAnnualOperatingCost}
        projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)}
      />
      <!-- DIRECTLY AFTER EXPENDITURES, and that placement is the argument for
           moving it out of the rail rather than just the place it fits. The
           section above says what the project is budgeted to cost and what it
           has SPENT against that; this one says who put the budget up. They are
           two halves of the money on
           this project, and reading them back to back is how "$359,040 of
           $528,000" becomes a fact about three named funders instead of two
           numbers. In the rail they were a screen apart and in different
           columns.

           It also gets its width back. Four columns — two of them long
           organization names, two non-wrapping figures — had 22rem in the rail
           and overflowed it; here they have the main column, which is what the
           table was designed against. -->
      <Firma2ProjectFunding
        funding={detail.funding}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
      />
      <Firma2ProgramRequirements detail={detail} />
      <Firma2ProjectTimeline milestones={detail.milestones} />

      <!-- AFTER THE TRACKED DATA — photos are CONTEXT for the numbers, not an
           answer to the page's question, so they follow everything tracked. A
           card, because a photo grid does not get better with every pixel —
           three tiles abreast at caption-readable size is its natural extent.
           The map that used to follow them is in the rail now (see the module
           header); the column no longer lands on a surface, so the comments
           can close it. -->
      <Firma2ProjectGallery photos={detail.photos} />

      <!-- LAST: the conversation, the way an activity feed closes an issue.
           It was the rail's third tab; it is READ, not set, so it belongs in
           the document, and it belongs at the end because every note is about
           something above it. firma2-project-comments has the full argument
           and the count-in-heading rule. -->
      <Firma2ProjectComments comments={detail.comments} />
    </div>
    </div>
  </EsaContainer>
  </div>
  </div>
</AppLayout>
